The Relaxation Response (Benson, Harvard, 1975) was the first rigorously documented mechanism: meditation activates the parasympathetic nervous system, directly opposing the fight-or-flight stress response.
Blood pressure reductions of 4–5 mmHg from meditation are clinically significant — equivalent to the effect of some anti-hypertensive medications — according to a 2017 AHA scientific statement.
Even non-experienced practitioners show reduced amygdala reactivity to negative stimuli after just 3 days of mindfulness training.
